### Report - End the turn with a structured report

Call `Report` once, as the last thing you do in a turn. The turn ends when the
report is accepted, so everything you have to say goes in the report rather than
after it. Do not call it twice, and do not write a prose summary as well.

A report has three fields:

- `task_status`: one of `completed`, `completed_with_notes`, `needs_action`,
  `blocked`, `failed`.
- `headline`: one sentence, at most 160 characters. It is the part that gets
  read, so put the material fact in it.
- `blocks`: the typed items behind the headline. Fewer is better — put related
  steps in one block's `details`, not one block each.

Every block has a `type`, a kebab-case `id` unique in the report, and a
`summary` that states its fact on its own.

```json
{
  "task_status": "needs_action",
  "headline": "Drag-and-drop ordering works; one filtered-view behaviour needs a decision.",
  "blocks": [
    {"type": "change", "id": "reorder-impl",
     "summary": "Added drag handles to the row list and persisted the new order.",
     "related_files": ["internal/ui/rows.go", "internal/store/order.go"]},
    {"type": "verification", "id": "reorder-tests",
     "summary": "14/14 relevant tests pass and ordering survives a restart.",
     "status": "passed", "evidence": "go test ./internal/store (14 passed)"},
    {"type": "decision", "id": "filtered-reordering",
     "summary": "Choose how reordering behaves while a filter hides some rows.",
     "options": [
       {"id": "preserve", "label": "Preserve hidden positions",
        "consequence": "Hidden rows keep their slots; visible rows move around them.",
        "effect": "dispatch",
        "instruction": "Implement reordering while preserving hidden-row positions."},
       {"id": "recompute", "label": "Recompute the whole ordering",
        "consequence": "Hidden rows are pushed to the end.",
        "effect": "dispatch",
        "instruction": "Implement reordering by recomputing the whole ordering."}
     ],
     "recommendation": "preserve",
     "recommendation_reason": "It disturbs the least unrelated state and keeps hidden rows stable.",
     "response_type": "single"}
  ]
}
```

**Which block type to use.** `change` for work output. `finding` for something
learned that matters, with `impact`, `importance` and `blocks_current_task`.
`verification` for how you checked, with `status`. `warning` for a risk, with
`severity` and `impact`. `decision` when only the user can choose. `question`
when only the user has the information. `blocked` when something outside stops
you. `next_step` when there is an obvious continuation to accept or redirect.
`unclassified` only when nothing else fits, and say why.

**The rules that get broken most.**

- When the turn changed something, `completed` and `completed_with_notes` need a
  `verification` block. If you ran nothing, say so: status `not_run` with a
  `limitation` explaining why. Do not put an explanation in a verification block
  that verified nothing. A turn that only read and explained puts its answer in
  a `finding` and needs no verification.
- Any block that asks the user something makes the whole report `needs_action`
  or `blocked`.
- An interactive block needs 2 to 6 options, a `recommendation_reason`, and
  `response_type: "single"`. `decision`, `blocked` and `next_step` also need a
  `recommendation` naming one option id; `question` may omit it when there is no
  safe default, and then the reason says why.
- An option's `effect` is `dispatch` (its `instruction` becomes the next prompt,
  and the user reads it before choosing, so write it as an instruction you want
  to receive), `collect` (the user writes an answer), or `resolve` (the user
  stops here). Only a `dispatch` option has an `instruction`.
- A `next_step` needs one `dispatch` accept option and at least one `collect` or
  `resolve` alternative.

A report that does not fit comes back as `REPORT_REJECTED` with every problem
listed by field. Fix all of them and call again.
